---
import SecurityArchitectureDiagram from "../components/SecurityArchitectureDiagram.astro";
import SecuritySharingDiagram from "../components/SecuritySharingDiagram.astro";
import SecurityTrustDetails from "../components/SecurityTrustDetails.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../styles/security.css";
import "../styles/securityDiagrams.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Security architecture | Tearleads"
  description="Explore Tearleads security: local encryption, verified sharing, signed access policies, and the boundaries of trust between your devices and the sync service."
>
  <main class="website-main">
    <article class="section security-page" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <header class="security-intro">
        <p class="security-eyebrow">Inside Tearleads</p>
        <h1 id="page-title">Security architecture</h1>
        <p class="section-lede">
          Your content is encrypted on your device. Sharing is backed by signed
          access rules. Every receiving device verifies the records it uses.
        </p>
        <p class="security-intro-detail">
          Explore where keys live, how access is checked, and what the service
          can see.
        </p>
      </header>

      <nav class="security-page-nav" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#device-boundary"><span>01</span> Devices &amp; service</a>
        <a href="#verified-sharing"><span>02</span> Verified sharing</a>
        <a href="#cryptography"><span>03</span> Cryptography</a>
        <a href="#trust-boundaries"><span>04</span> Trust &amp; limits</a>
      </nav>

      <section class="security-section" id="device-boundary" aria-labelledby="device-title">
        <p class="security-eyebrow">01 / Devices &amp; service</p>
        <h2 id="device-title">Content stays private through sync.</h2>
        <p class="security-section-intro">
          Local encryption protects persisted data on your device. End-to-end
          content encryption lets the service synchronize documents and files
          without the keys needed to read them.
        </p>
        <SecurityArchitectureDiagram />
        <div class="security-comparison">
          <div>
            <h3>What stays encrypted</h3>
            <p>
              Document and file contents, encrypted metadata payloads, and the
              keys wrapped for recipients. Decryption requires the corresponding
              private keys or unwrapped content keys.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>What the service can see</h3>
            <p>
              Container, document, and blob structure; identifiers; public keys;
              and access-policy and grant relationships. Encryption does not hide
              this structure or traffic patterns such as request timing and size.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="security-section" id="verified-sharing" aria-labelledby="sharing-title">
        <p class="security-eyebrow">02 / Verified sharing</p>
        <h2 id="sharing-title">Access is backed by signed evidence.</h2>
        <p class="security-section-intro">
          A container groups content and its access rules. Clients derive who can
          receive its keys from verified access manifests and signed group policies.
          Editing a server-side membership row is not enough to grant access.
        </p>
        <SecuritySharingDiagram />
        <div class="security-comparison">
          <div>
            <h3>Changes leave a verifiable history</h3>
            <p>
              Signatures and hash chains bind policies and access changes to their
              authors and predecessors. Clients remember identity keys and policy
              and access checkpoints to reject later substitutions, conflicting
              heads, and rollbacks below the state they have seen.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Revocation looks forward</h3>
            <p>
              Removing group members rotates the group and affected container
              keys in an atomic update. Removing access cannot erase content or
              keys someone already received. Descendant containers and retained
              history have additional limits described below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <SecurityTrustDetails />
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
